** all four pass. The word unit suites ran 42/42 and the common ones 7/7. Both differential oracles (original against new) passed 3 cases each, with every compared output identical, including the ~100-page large.docx.\n- **Repaint scope:** I re-ran `word-repaint.js` on the 100-page document. It shows the same pattern PKT-0109 found in round 2. Every local keystroke clears all page caches once, and every remote change twice. 2 of 20 remote changes fell back to recalculating the whole document.\n- **No speed gain from round 2:** the fork is still at 0f61ccc. Round 2 delivered a measurement script and an oracle case, but no sdkjs change.\n- **Timing noise:** in the differential, the new code opened large.docx about 40% faster than the original. The only code difference is the round 1 font fix, worth about 7%, so I recorded this as noise from run order and caching, not as a gain.\n\n**Verdict: refactor, same language, now aimed at opening documents.**\n- **Ordered by user time:** the round 3 journey maps put 471–1 250 ms per open in this module, against 3–16 ms per keystroke or remote change. The biggest single stage is `layout-finish-paint`: 1 250 ms of the 4 628 ms it takes to open the 100-page document (27%).\n- **One plan row (P1):** write `word-layout-window.js` to break that post-load layout window into computing, idle time between timer slices, and painting. It also measures how slow typing feels during that window, and gives the possible gain of each fix before anyone writes one.\n- **Not re-planned:** typing and co-editing (`paint`, `b-paint`, `b-apply`) are listed as `not_covered` because their fixes are already pending as PKT-0144 and PKT-0145. Splitting `client-open-word` is left to PKT-0133, also pending.\n- **Nothing else changed:** there was no round 2 `-b` file to remove, and I did not prototype anything in `external/sdkjs`.\n\n**Lessons for the Atlas:**\n- The journey stage times settled the plan order immediately.\n- The differential's timing lines can